---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Bug1 = `age = input("How old are you? ")

print("Next year you will be " + age + 1)`;

const Bug2 = `price = int(input("What did it cost? "))

print("With tax:", price * 1.07)`;

const Bug3 = `total = int(input("Total points scored: "))
players = int(input("How many players? "))

print("Points each:", total / players)`;

const Bug4 = `first = input("First number: ")
second = input("Second number: ")

print("Sum:", first + second)`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Debug Challenge: Runtime &amp; Casting</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Four more broken programs. This time they all involve <b>input</b>{" "}
            and <b>casting</b>, which means three of them crash while running rather
            than refusing to start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Activity 1.8 named the three kinds of mistakes. Use those names here.
          </p>

          <h4>Skills to Practice</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>Reading a traceback</li>
            <li>Telling a runtime error from a logic error</li>
            <li>Knowing when and what to cast</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="reminder">
          <h2>The Three Kinds, Again</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Syntax error</b> &mdash; Python cannot read
              it. Nothing runs.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Runtime error</b> &mdash; it starts, then
              crashes partway through. You get a traceback.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Logic error</b> &mdash; it runs all the way
              through and gives you the wrong answer, with no error at all.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>One of the four below is a logic error.</b> Deciding which one is
            most of the work.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bugs">
          <h2>The Four Programs</h2>

          <p>
            Run each one, then write down which kind of mistake it is, what is
            actually wrong, and the corrected line.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 1</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> enter <b>16</b> and it crashes. The traceback ends with
            a <b class="text-sn-red">TypeError</b>.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug1}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="Bug 1"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Careful &mdash; there are two <b class="text-sn-cyan">+</b> signs on
            that line, and they are not doing the same job. Which one is the
            problem?
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 2</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> enter <b>20</b> and it works fine. Enter <b>12.99</b>{" "}
            and it crashes with a <b class="text-sn-red">ValueError</b>.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug2}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="Bug 2"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            A program that only breaks on <i>some</i> inputs is still broken. What
            kind of number is a price?
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 3</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> works for most inputs. Enter <b>0</b> players and it
            crashes with a <b class="text-sn-red">ZeroDivisionError</b>.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug3}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="Bug 3"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            This one is different from the others: the casting is correct and the
            math is correct. Describe in words what the program would have to{" "}
            <i>check</i> before dividing. You do not have the tool to fix it
            properly yet &mdash; that arrives in Activity 1.10.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 4</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Symptom:</b> enter <b>3</b> and <b>5</b>. It prints{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-red">Sum: 35</b>. No crash, no error message, and the
            answer should be <b class="text-sn-green">8</b>.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Bug4}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="Bug 4"
            showLineNumbers={true}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Explain <i>why</i> it printed 35 rather than 8. The program did exactly
            what it was told &mdash; that is what makes this kind of bug dangerous.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For each of the four bugs, submit:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Which kind of mistake it is &mdash; syntax, runtime, or logic</li>
            <li>One sentence on what was wrong</li>
            <li>The corrected line, or for Bug 3 a description of the fix</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Bugs 1, 2, and 3 all handed you a traceback. Bug 4 handed you nothing.
            Which one would you have been least likely to notice on your own?
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Debugging
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
